  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe central idea is to use adaptive grid refinement with a stochastic gradient descent (SGD) approach. Unlike previous strategies that rely solely on local search, this method combines global exploration via SGD and faster-than-linear convergence in fine grids. The implementation computes Kriging-based surrogate models (Gaussian Processes) to better estimate the function landscape than random walks. This approach addresses the challenge of balancing global exploration with local refinement by maintaining a hyper-rectangle of probable optimal solutions and dynamically adjusting the grid resolution. Key limitations include potential numerical instability in highly correlated dimensions, but surrogate models help mitigate this.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nRepresentation: h(x) is modeled as a discretized step function on uniform grid points in [0,2].\n\nObjective: minimize C5(h) = max_k \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx. The grid n is doubled adaptively from 360 to 1440 points.\n\nConstraints:\n- Box constraints: 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1\n- Integral constraint: sum(h) * dx = 1 (with dx=2/n)\n\nAlgorithm:\nFor a grid size n:\n- Initialize with projected gradient descent using stochastic estimates of the gradient\n- Maintain a hyperrectangle R of feasible solutions approximating the optimum\n- Adaptive grid refinement: transfer parent solutions to finer grids and optimize locally\n- Uses Kriging surrogate predictions to guide the stochastic gradient\n\n3. Implementation procedure\ndef run(seed=42, budget_s=1100, **kwargs):\n    # Parent initialization\n    if \"initial_h_values\" in globals():\n        parent = np.array(initial_h_values, dtype=float)\n    else:\n        parent = random_feasible_vector(360, np.random)\n    \n    budgets = [360, 720, 1440]\n    for i in range(len(budgets)):\n        n = budgets[i]\n        h = interpolate_parent(parent, n/n if i>0 else 1.0)\n        h = enforce_sum(h, n/2, np.random)\n        \n        # Adaptive SGD with Kriging surrogate\n        h = kriging_sgd_optimize(h, n, time_budget=budget_s/len(budgets))\n        \n        # Record best candidate for propagation to next grid\n        best = h.copy()\n        parent = best\n    \n    return best.tolist(), compute_c5(best), len(best)\n\ndef kriging_sgd_optimize(h, n, time_budget):\n    # Set up Kriging model components\n    kernel = RBFKernel()\n    optimizer = BoundableOptimizer(range(len(h)), (0,1), lengthscale=0.6)\n    \n    # Initialize hyperrectangle\n    lower_bound = np.clip(h-0.05, 0, 1)\n    upper_bound = np.clip(h+0.05, 0, 1)\n    hyperrect = [lower_bound, upper_bound]\n    \n    # Function evaluations for initialization\n    n_evals = max(5, min(20, int(0.05/time_budget)))\n    for _ in range(n_evals):\n        x_opt = np.clip(kernel.sample_smoothing(nan_stats), 0, 1)\n        f_val = compute_c5(x_opt)\n        # Update Kriging model and hyperrect bounds\n        ...\n\n    time_remaining = time_budget - time.time_prev()\n    iterations = int(0.8 * time_remaining)\n    for it in range(iterations):\n        # Sample from hyperrectangle\n        x_sample = hyperrect[0] + (hyperrect[1]-hyperrect[0]) * np.random.rand(len(h))\n        \n        # Gradient estimation via Anderson acceleration\n        grad_est = stochastic_gradient(x_sample, n)\n        \n        # Stochastic gradient descent step\n        x_new = project_to_bounds(\n            x_sample - 0.05 * (np.random.rand(len(h)) < 0.5) * grad_est,\n            (0,1)\n        )\n        \n        # New function evaluation\n        f_val_new = compute_c5(x_new)\n        \n        # Update hyperrect bounds\n        update_hyperrect(lower_bound, upper_bound, x_new, f_val_new, ...)\n        \n        # Update Kriging model\n        for landmark in krig_landmarks:\n            update_kernel_predictions(landmark, x_new, f_val_new)\n    \n    return x_opt\n\n4. Search choices and variations\nKey hyperparameters:\n- Grid selection: Starts from 360, then 720, then 1440 if time allows\n- Kriging lengthscale: base=0.6, adapted based on grid density\n- Number of initial evaluations: ranges from 5 to 20\n- Gradient learning rate: 0.05 to 0.15\n- Penalty for bounds violations: adaptive\n\nImportant variations:\n- Use Anderson acceleration for gradient-free optimization\n- Dynamic grid adjustment based on time_remaining\n- Initialization by projecting onto thermal neighborhood of parent\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\nEstimation for n=1440:\n- Correlation evaluation: O(n^2) per lag? Modeled complexity is O(n)\n- Rough estimate: 1440^2 = 2 million operations per full correlation, which is manageable but time-intensive\n- Total budget: 1100 seconds\n- Stage breakdown:\n  - Initial: linear warmup\n  - SGD iterations: 5 minutes at n=1440\n  - Cooldown: hyperrect refinement\n\n6. Validation and return contract\nReturned h must satisfy:\n- 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i\n- abs(sum(h)*dx - 1) < 1e-6\n- compute_c5(h) correctly calculated\n- C5 strictly better than current record\n\nCritical functions implemented:\ndef enforce_sum(h, target, rng):\n    # Clip and adjust\n    ...\n\ndef compute_c5(h):\n    # Only uses mode=\"full\" \n    # Returns: n/2 * max_corr? No, use the exact scaling from the original\n    ...",
